  "strategy": "**Approach and rationale:**  \nThis strategy uses a multi-stage optimization process that analyzes the autocorrelation structure and constriction patterns to iteratively minimize the objective. Starting from a base sequence, it applies convolution-based regularization, subgradient projection, and perturbation exploration. The key idea is to penalize sequences with high convolution maxima through structure-aware constraints while scaling them to maintain non-negativity and sum with a probabilistic direction search. The approach dynamically adjusts to solver progress, avoiding local minima by introducing hierarchical refinements. The algorithm balances the peak minimization (via a weighting kernel) and support optimization, with incremental checks to ensure meaningful improvements.\n\n**Mathematical specification:**  \nThe sequence `a` has length `n`, and the objective is:  \n\\[\n\\mathrm{objective} = \\frac{2n \\cdot \\|\\mathbf{a} \\ast \\mathbf{a}\\|_{\\infty}}{\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|^2_{1}}\n\\]  \nProjected onto the $(n)$-simplex. Constraints enforce non-negativity and element-wise bounds. Each evaluation requires finite-max convolution via FFT.\n\n**Implementation procedure:**  \n1. Initialization: Load the parent sequence if available, or start with a simplex sample.  \n2.Convolutional regularization: For each candidate, compute its autocorrelation via `compute_conv`, then raise an L-infinity constraint based on the maximum value.  \n3.Layer-wise optimization (gradient-free): Use L1-regularized sampling to guide structure prioritization.  \n4.Adaptive subgradient refinement: Re-weight LEAST significant features by factorizing the convolution matrix.  \n5.Dynamic timeline assignment: Manage job interruptions by caching improvements and allowing restarts.  \n\n**Search choices and useful variations:**  \nThe fixed length is \\(n = 1500\\). Start with high-regularity tests to ensure global minima are found; then scale down for faster convergence. Allow line initialization at \\(n/2\\) for better sampling. Adaptive kernel size. Terminate if a local minimum is found.  \n\n**Compute budget and robustness:**  \nEnsure all solver calls respect the time budget. Use up to 30% time for core constraints. Stop early if progress stalls.  \n\n**Validation and return contract:**  \nPerform an exact evaluation on the best sequence. Return a finite-max sequence of explicit nonnegative floats.",
